<http://w3id.org/attack/attack-pattern--dfe29258-ce59-421c-9dee-e85cb9fa90cd> a <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#AttackPattern> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#CourseOfAction> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#Collection> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#Relationship> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#Malware> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#Tool> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#IntrusionSet> ;
	<http://schema.org/name> "Lockscreen Bypass" ;
	<http://schema.org/description> "Techniques have periodically been demonstrated that exploit vulnerabilities on Android (Citation: Wired-AndroidBypass), iOS (Citation: Kaspersky-iOSBypass), or other mobile devices to bypass the device lock screen. The vulnerabilities are generally patched by the device/operating system vendor once they become aware of their existence.  Platforms: Android, iOS" ;
