<http://w3id.org/attack/attack-pattern--3911658a-6506-4deb-9ab4-595a51ae71ad> a <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#AttackPattern> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#CourseOfAction> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#Collection> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#Relationship> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#Malware> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#Tool> , <http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#IntrusionSet> ;
	<http://schema.org/name> "Commonly Used Port" ;
	<http://schema.org/description> "Adversaries may communicate over a commonly used port to bypass firewalls or network detection systems and to blend with normal network activity to avoid more detailed inspection. They may use commonly open ports such as * TCP:80 (HTTP) * TCP:443 (HTTPS) * TCP:25 (SMTP) * TCP/UDP:53 (DNS)  They may use the protocol associated with the port or a completely different protocol.  Platforms: Android, iOS" ;
	<http://w3id.org/sepses/vocab/ref/attack#platform> "Android" , "iOS" ;
